## Library Overview 
<hr style="width:100%;border-bottom:3px solid black;">

{#A Standard Library for Makefiles#}

**In library-mode,** `compose.mk` is used as an `include` in a project Makefile (see the [installation docs]({{mkdocs.site_relative_url}}/quickstart) for more details).  After including `compose.mk`, you can access a huge library of make-targets and macros.  

This page focuses on **reusable static targets**, as opposed to [scaffolded](({{mkdocs.site_relative_url}}/bridge) or [otherwise dynamic]{{mkdocs.site_relative_url}}/demos/polyglots/#foreign-code-as-first-class) targets.  Macros/functions are a different thing, [covered here]({{mkdocs.site_relative_url}}/macros).

What's really interesting about targets is that they do double duty as a structured API that's both **internal and external**.

> Internal, in the sense that you can use them as part of normal prereqs inside your project Makefile.  External, in the sense that you can call them as goals, driving `compose.mk` from another scripting context.

Because `make` has never really had a standard library[^1], there's lots of pretty basic stuff that is conspicuously missing.  For quick examples of the boring simple stuff:

* [Logging and colored output](#logging-facilities) 
* [Online help]({{mkdocs.site_relative_url}}/cli-help) for listing and inspecting available automation
* [Simple Assertions](#assertion-support)

